f near-identical rejected proposals is itself a defect, not diligence. Step 3: Review the auto-memory files for: 1) Duplicate or redundant entries (same learning stated multiple ways), 2) Stale information (outdated facts, deprecated workflows), 3) Low-value noise (trivial observations that don't warrant long-term memory), 4) Missing structure (unclear categories, hard to navigate). If you find significant issues (>10% of the auto-memory content should be removed/refactored), create an evolution proposal for cleanup THERE (auto-memory), never against .instar/MEMORY.md. ALWAYS write a handoff note at the end summarizing total word count across auto-memory files, entry count, and hygiene status: > .instar/state/job-handoff-memory-hygiene.md. IMPORTANT: If the auto-memory corpus exceeds 15000 words, this is a critical finding regardless of organization quality \u2014 identify the lowest-value 20% and create an evolution proposal for removal there."
